Q: Declaring an object before initializing it in c++ A: <p>There is a way to do this without pointers/heap memory, this syntax is just a bit gibberish. Here is an example using std::string. I don't recommend doing this unless you really need the performance.</p>
<pre><code>alignas(std::string) uint8_t object[sizeof(std::string)];

int main() {
    if(true)
        new(&amp;object) std::string(&quot;Your arguments&quot;);
    else
        new(&amp;object) std::string(&quot;Your other arguments&quot;);

    (*(std::string*)(&amp;object)).append(&quot;&quot;);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; (*(std::string*)(&amp;object));
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The annoying part about this is you have to cast object to a string every time you want to use it:</p>
<pre><code>(*(std::string*)(&amp;object))
</code></pre>
<p>Edit: fixed alignment</p>
